\f1\fs22 Piotr Dunhilton ate a breakfast of steak tartare and threw a bottle of milk at his cat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91This is my breakfast, you mangey animal,\'92 he lectured intemperately as the cat scuttled away to lick her coat clean.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The meat he chewed in his mouth was no longer a comfort for the splenetic carnivorous beast of his power-hungry soul. It felt like an oily chewed sponge; he spat it back on his plate.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Get me the Commissioner of Police,\'92 he told his tablet.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Calling Very Hardman
\f1\i0 , it responded. After a few seconds, a coffee cup and Gerry\'92s face appeared on its thumb-smeared screen.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Morning CEO,\'92 Gerry answered. Without his tablet tagging the caller, he might not have recognised Dunhilton. He had left his tablet on the kitchen bench so its camera caught little more than his exaggerated flabby chin and prominent Adam\'92s apple.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You\'92ve been briefed?\'92 Dunhilton ensured he sounded terse, hoping to avoid the ignominy of the complete cluster tax of the previous evening.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Indeed, your Serene Corporateness.\'92 Gerry slurped coffee. It sounded milky; Dunhilton never trusted anyone who didn\'92t drink coffee as black as their heart.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Tell me some good news. Please tell me, this time we\'92ve caught the real Nightclub Vampire.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It seems\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91This serial killer\'92s presence at a party celebrating the holy joint venture of a loving, calculating Airborne couple\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s a serious lapse in intelligence on PAPER\'92s part,\'92 Gerry led.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91PAPER\'92s part? Civil order is a police matter,\'92 Dunhilton said icily.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Of course, your Serene Corporateness, in the same way a covertly planned disruption of an Airborne society event is an intelligence concern, especially where the Elvi are concerned,\'92 Gerry said with care. \'91I think PAPER has seriously dropped the spyglass here.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Very Hardman had Dunhilton there.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There are also disturbing aspects regarding the Nightclub Vampire \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton released a quiet, high-pitched keening wail and blinked. \'91Hook in the Director of Freedom,\'92 he commanded his tablet. \'91The new Director, not the retired one.\'92\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Calling Heston Whats-His-Name
\f1\i0 , the tablet responded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>After new Freedom joined the meeting and minimal greetings were performed, Dunhilton asked for an update on the Nightclub Vampire case.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91That\'92s police\'92s area,\'92 new Freedom deflected.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, Gerry?\'92 Dunhilton asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heston the new Director of Freedom noted the CEO\'92s familiarity with the Commissioner of Police, and was jealous. He swallowed hard.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91The identity of the killer is a delicate matter,\'92 Gerry said. \'91Perhaps the Director of Freedom may be more illuminating than me \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heston recognised the threat and decided to get ahead of it. \'91He\'92s one of ours, a deep-cover PAPER agent.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91One of yours, Heston?\'92 Dunhilton wheezed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heston noted the CEO\'92s familiarity with him, and was terrified. He swallowed even harder. \'91Gone rogue, obviously,\'92 he said and almost choked on his words.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton scrolled scrolled through reports looking for a profile on the killer. Nothing. Not even a name or a picture. \'91Who was this guy?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91His cover name was Haloren Smythe, your Serene Corporateness,\'92 Heston offered, hoping any information would mollify the CEO. \'91There\'92s quite a long chain of black ops involved here. CEO, it\'92s taking some time to unpick. I am yet to be read in on any of this. and all of it stems from long before my appointment as Director.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91This \'85 Haloren Smythe struck again on your watch, Heston,\'92 Dunhilton said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>If Heston was not already feeling enough heat, Gerry added, \'91A shame about the previous director, hugging a pillow like he did.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heston gulped. His predecessor had \'91hugged a pillow\'92\'97a favorite method of executing disgraced public servants, throwing them out a top-floor window and letting the Layer eat at them before whatever was left splattered on the filthy Cloudborne ground. What could be worse?: being thrown out a lower window, that\'92s what. The lower the window, the closer to the Layer, especially so the plummeting condemned could achieve\'97with apologies for the pun\'97terminal velocity, the longer it would take to pass through the Layer and the longer its caustic chemicals could perform their desired function. The slowest and longest passage through the Layer could be achieved by ejecting the victim out a window just above the Layer. If the sun was just right, the Layer could look like pillow-like fluffy cloud with a silver lining that could dissolve teeth. The victim would flop out the window as if if landing on a pillow, hence \'91pillowing\'92.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What do you know of Smythe\'92s assignments?\'92 Gerry prompted Heston, already knowing the answer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91An operation has been running for \'85 well, many decades \'85 a white collar forensics op searching for hidden assets of long-dead billionaires. Their mission has two prongs. One is: recover assets and claim them for the great Airborne Meritocracy of Ulyssia; the other: find any potential claimants for the assets and neutralise them.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton digested. \'91You mean, find any potential inheritors and kill them.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, CEO.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Which side of the operation was Smythe on?\'92 Dunhilton asked, expecting Smythe would be one of those \'91neutralising\'92 potential inheritors.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91He was on the financial forensic side of the operation. His code name was Midas, apparently because\'97\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Everything he touched \'85\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, your Serene Corporateness.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry injected, \'91He was in the neighbourhood of the 
\f2\i Stickett Inn
\f1\i0  when it was bombed,\'92 and allowed the corner of his mouth to curl with a mischievous smile. He watched Heston wilt.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton tried to hide his glee. \'91We caught the Nightclub Vampire 
\f2\i and
\f1\i0  the bomber?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Is that right, Freedom?\'92 Gerry slurped his coffee.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heston felt caught in a trap. He stammered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, Heston?\'92 Dunhilton prompted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Smythe struck the night of the bombing, in the vicinity of the 
\f2\i Stickett Inn
\f1\i0 .\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, I suppose that is some swift justice,\'92 Dunhilton remarked. \'91Who was the bomber?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Her name was Avi Grubler,\'92 Gerry answered for Heston, who squirmed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton zeroed in on Heston\'92s discomfort. \'91What?\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heston reluctantly said, \'91She was also one of ours.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That took Dunhilton some moments to process. \'91You mean a civil servant?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Like a rodent watches a cat, Heston watched Gerry and, when it seemed Gerry might answer on his behalf, he admitted, \'91A PAPER agent.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Even via tablet conference, the other two might have heard Dunhilton\'92s gears ceasing. His impatience escalated. Of all the things\'97the Elvi gatecrashing a holy joint venture service and the reception after \'85 who was this High Elvis? \'85 who is the Holy Momma? \'85 Sarah Jacobson? \'85 was an Airborne leading an insurgency against social stability in Ulyssia?\'97Dunhilton expected the Nightclub Vampire case would be the easiest to square away \'85\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'85 it would have been cleaner if Smythe had succeeded in murdering Sarah Jacobson before that idiot Alexander Koalemos had caught him in the act. \'91You mean a PAPER agent murdered a PAPER agent? Why?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heston chewed his words for a few seconds before he spat them out. \'91Smythe and Grubler were working on the same operation\'97Smythe on finding the assets, Grubler on neutralising potential heirs.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Did they know each other?\'92 Dunhilton asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Unlikely,\'92 Heston answered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry took a different tack. \'91Grubler blew up a bar to assassinate a potential heir; that seems rather excessive.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton shrugged. \'91It was a Cloudborne bar. The thing I find incredulous is, why would an Airborne travel under the Layer in the first place?\'92 That Grubler\'92s target might be Cloudborne occurred to none of them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91CEO,\'92 Gerry said delicately. \'91There\'92s another wrinkle regarding Smythe.\'92 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton huffed. \'91Spit it out, Commissioner.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Neither I nor, I dare say, Heston can say for certain that Smythe is dead.\'92 Gerry waited for Dunhilton\'92s bluster; none blowed. Gerry cleared his throat. \'91His body is missing.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91How the tax do you lose a body?\'92 Dunhilton demanded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heston permitted himself the brief luxury of feeling smug.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91There is no record of the ambulance ever delivering Haloren Smythe to any hospital or morgue,\'92 Gerry said. \'91It disappeared off the grid for a number of hours, then returned to the station, without Smythe\'92s body.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Then surely you\'92ve interviewed the drivers.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They\'92ve also disappeared. Their contact details are fake, dead tablet numbers, home addresses are vacant lots or burned-down Memphis fried chicken joints.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We have nothing on them?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Well, there is one line we\'92re pursuing, CEO. Their names were given as Dick van Dyke and Rick von Reich. Those names are known to police. They\'92re frequently used by part-time PAPER grunts.\'92
